  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The height <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi></math> through which a liquid will rise in a capillary tube of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math> is given by,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>T</mi><mi>cos</mi><mi>&#952;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>&#961;</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#8733;</mo><mfrac><mi>T</mi><mi>&#961;</mi></mfrac><mo>&#8733;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#961;</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>As we can see in both cases, value of height will remain same. Therefore,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>'</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>05</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math></p>",
